 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n# Collections.abc objects not subscriptable in Python 3.9+\n\n## Description\n\nStarting with Python 3.9, certain objects in the collections.abc module should be subscriptable (like `collections.abc.MutableSet[int]`), but this functionality appears to be broken.\n\nWhen trying to use type annotations with collections.abc classes, the code fails to recognize them as subscriptable:\n\n```python\nimport collections.abc\ncollections.abc.MutableSet[int]  # This should work in Python 3.9+\n```\n\nThe issue also affects class inheritance when using generic type annotations:\n\n```python\nimport collections.abc\nclass Derived(collections.abc.Iterator[int]):\n    pass\n```\n\n## How to reproduce\n\nCreate a simple script that tries to use collections.abc classes with subscript notation:\n\n```python\nimport collections.abc\n\n# These should all work in Python 3.9+\nprint(collections.abc.MutableSet[int])\nprint(collections.abc.Iterator[str])\nprint(collections.abc.ByteString[int])\n\n# This should also work for inheritance\nclass MyIterator(collections.abc.Iterator[int]):\n    def __next__(self):\n        return 1\n    \n    def __iter__(self):\n        return self\n```\n\nWhen running this code, you'll get a TypeError indicating that the collections.abc objects are not subscriptable.\n\nThe issue appears to be related to the removal of the transformation code that adds the `__class_getitem__` method to these classes, which is necessary for them to support the subscript notation introduced in Python 3.9.\n</issue>\n",
